  "fromId": "s",
  "toId": "h",
  "preamble": "Debuccalization of the voiceless alveolar fricative [s] to the glottal fricative [h] removes the oral constriction and leaves only glottal friction. It is one of the most frequent consonant lenitions: it defines the Iranian branch (*saptá > hapta), Ancient Greek (heptá vs Latin septem), Brythonic Celtic (Welsh hen vs Irish sean), Eastern Polynesian (hina vs Samoan sina), and Buryat within Mongolic (hara 'moon'). In Quechuan, Proto-Quechuan *s became h in 'one' (*suk > huk) in several Quechua I varieties. Within Mon-Khmer, Kuy (Katuic) changed final *s to h (*ʔuus 'fire' > ʔuːh) and Plang (Palaungic) changed *s to h initially and finally (*suk 'hair' > hɤ́k, *muːs 'nose' > mɯ̀h). Armenian shows the same change: PIE *sénos 'old' > հին hin.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Debuccalization, Lenition",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Proto-Indo-Iranian to Proto-Iranian",
      "languageFamily": "Iranian",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Proto-Indo-Iranian *saptá 'seven'",
          "to": "Proto-Iranian *haptá",
          "note": "Iranian debuccalized *s to *h except after *k, *n, *p, *t or before *t; continued as Middle Persian haft, Balochi hapt, Northern Kurdish heft."
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Indo-Iranian *saȷ́ʰásram 'thousand'",
          "to": "Proto-Iranian *hajáhram",
          "note": "Both *s became *h; continued as Balochi hazár, Northern Kurdish hezar, Parthian hazār."
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Indo-Iranian *smáwčati 'releases'",
          "to": "Proto-Iranian *hmáwčati"
        }
      ]
    },
